See the end of the file for copying conditions.

Please send bug reports and suggestions through the project's issue tracker.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (2026-09-06)

This is the first release.  It runs as a single process: closing the terminal
ends the session, and detaching is not implemented.  See "Known limitations".

** Panes

Each pane is a process on a pseudo-terminal whose output is parsed into a
terminal grid and painted into the display.  Colours, bold, italic, underline,
inverse and double-width characters are carried through.  Resizing propagates
both to the kernel and to the parser, so programs inside see the size they are
actually given.

Mouse events reach the program in a pane when that program has asked for them,
and are otherwise interpreted by dirk.  A click means "focus this pane" in a
shell and "click this line" in a program that reports mouse input; there is no
mode to switch between the two.

** The sidebar

Static pages sit above the project tree.  A page is a singleton with no project
— one system monitor, one repository browser — spawned when first opened rather
than kept running in the background.  A page whose program is not on PATH is
dropped at startup rather than failing when it is opened.

Below the pages, a project appears once it has a workspace and disappears when
its last one closes.  Every row is a click target.

** Naming

Workspaces are named from the terminal title the program inside publishes, which
is how coding agents report what they are working on.  No model is called and no
credentials are required.

A label dirk did not write is never overwritten.  Placeholder labels are
adopted.  A reworded title describing the same work is not treated as new
intent, a title must hold still before it is committed, and one workspace is
renamed at most once per interval.  Shell prompts, bare paths, echoed command
lines and the plain repository name are never used as names.

** Configuration

dirk runs with no configuration file.  ~/.config/dirk/config.toml overrides
what it names and leaves the rest alone: the brand shown in the bar, where the
project picker looks, the sidebar width, the scrollback depth, the shell, the
list of pages, and the naming thresholds.

** Known limitations

Sessions do not survive the terminal closing.  There is no scrollback view and
no copy mode.  Splits divide a workspace into equal columns or equal rows and
do not nest.  The state glyph beside a workspace is inferred from whether the
pane has ever published a title, so an agent waiting on a human is not
distinguished from one that is working.  Keybindings are not configurable.

Each of these is recorded in the roadmap; see ROADMAP.md.

